<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!-- RUT_ComplexStructures — owner, 2026-09-07.

       He asked for "another graphical mutator that clearly identified 'complex
       structures' ... indicating a particularly complicated and dense network on the
       map (as there are in many, many places here)."

       🔑 IT HAS TO BE A LANDMARK, NOT A MUTATOR. Measured off the live defs this
       date: LandmarkDef carries iconTexturePath / drawScale / drawType /
       drawAboveRoads, and TileMutatorDef has NO visual fields at all — a mutator
       never draws anything on the world map. That is also the root cause of the
       complaint that prompted this: the Rust Cathedral averages 5.25 mutators per
       tile and every tile carries at least one, yet only 8 of its 236 tiles showed an
       icon, so a dense place read as barren.

       WHAT IT MEANS, and why it is honest. This landmark is placed where the tile
       ALREADY carries 6 or more mutators — the top ~1.2% of the planet (261 of
       21,872; measured, whole-planet distribution below). So the icon is not
       decoration asserting complexity, it is a readout of complexity that is really
       there. Owner chose the >=6 threshold from 6/7/8.

           0 muts 7730 | 1 7509 | 2 3715 | 3 1542 | 4 687 | 5 428
           6 146 | 7 86 | 8 22 | 9 3 | 10 2 | 11 1 | 12 1

       ⛔ commonality 0 ON PURPOSE. Worldgen must never roll this: its meaning comes
       entirely from the density rule, and a randomly-placed one would be a lie. It is
       placed only by ashkarr_place_complex_structures.py.

       ⛔ NO mutatorChances, also on purpose. A landmark's mutatorChances rolls go
       through AddMutator and can displace what is already on the tile — and these
       tiles are chosen precisely because they are already the busiest on the planet.
       Adding more would both risk the displacement and corrupt the very count the
       icon reports.

       Icon: four variants at 512px in a 1024 sheet (atlasSize 2,2), drawn by
       AshkarrLandmarkArt/make_complex_structures_icon.py in the house style —
       blocks wired together, so it reads as a NETWORK rather than as rubble
       (Ruins) or one compound (AncientGarrison). -->

  <LandmarkDef MayRequire="Ludeon.RimWorld.Odyssey">
    <defName>RUT_ComplexStructures</defName>
    <label>complex structures</label>
    <description>Not one ruin but a system of them: blocks, conduits and outbuildings still wired to each other long after whatever ran them stopped. Survey teams mark ground like this because it takes days to walk and longer to understand — whatever was built here was built to work together, and a great deal of it is still standing.</description>
    <iconTexturePath>World/Landmarks/Ashkarr/RUT_ComplexStructures</iconTexturePath>
    <atlasSize>(2, 2)</atlasSize>
    <commonality>0</commonality>
    <!-- `structure` is what Ruins and AncientGarrison use; read off the live defs
         rather than guessed. The only categories in use are structure / mountain /
         lake. NamerLandmark_Ruins likewise reused rather than authoring a RulePackDef
         for one landmark — same call IshkoDarkLandmarks made. -->
    <category>structure</category>
    <nameMaker>NamerLandmark_Ruins</nameMaker>
  </LandmarkDef>

</Defs>
